WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.539
You write a cheque to a friend on Friday. Your notebook already shows the money gone. But the bank only takes it out when your friend pays it in. For a few days, the two records differ.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:27.007
A bank reconciliation statement explains these timing differences. There are two: unpresented cheques, which the bank has not paid yet, and outstanding lodgements, which the bank has not added yet.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:41.296
Start from the updated cash book balance. Add unpresented cheques, because the bank has not taken that money out yet. Deduct outstanding lodgements, because the bank has not added them yet.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:54.156
An overdraft is a negative balance, so write it in brackets and use the same rules. An overdraft of five hundred, plus an unpresented cheque of three hundred, minus an outstanding lodgement of one hundred, gives an overdraft of three hundred.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:01:03.200
The result must equal the bank statement balance in the question. If you start from the bank statement instead, the signs reverse.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:18.600
Here is a real question from June 2024. It follows on from Nabil's updated cash book. Prepare a bank reconciliation statement at thirty April. Four marks.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:31.565
The bank statement shows a credit balance of three hundred and eighty-six. That is our target. Fatima's cheque of two hundred and forty-eight, and cash sales of eight hundred and ninety banked, are the timing differences.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:41.171
Start with the updated cash book balance of one thousand and twenty-eight. Fatima's cheque has not been paid by the bank yet, so add two hundred and forty-eight.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:59.300
The cash sales of eight hundred and ninety are not on the statement yet, so deduct them. One thousand and twenty-eight plus two hundred and forty-eight, minus eight hundred and ninety, is three hundred and eighty-six. It matches the bank statement. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:09.295
A common mistake is swapping the signs when starting from the cash book. Unpresented cheques are added, outstanding lodgements deducted.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:16.685
Another is putting standing orders or bank charges in the statement. They go in the cash book first.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:28.911
Now you try. Prepare Hana's bank reconciliation statement, and check it against the bank statement. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:44.960
Start at two thousand three hundred. Add the two unpresented cheques, four hundred and twenty and one hundred and eighty. Deduct the lodgement of six hundred and fifty. The result is two thousand two hundred and fifty, the same as the bank statement.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:55.560
Remember: start from the updated cash book, add unpresented cheques, deduct outstanding lodgements, and check against the statement. Now try the practice questions.
